{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1894 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8611c5025d5922f49487a71b257468b747f66b8a54b26382a320fa0cc659f732" :edb/db-before-t 1893 :edb/db-after-t 1894 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535206 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:31:56.489Z" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133617 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$." 13194139535206 false] [17592186133617 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133617 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨1, 0, 1⟩ \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(0)) \\\\\n&= f(e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}) - f(1, 0, 1) \\\\\n&= (e(1)^{2} + 2 \\cdot e \\cdot \\frac{1}{e}) - (1(0)^{2} + 2(1)(1)) \\\\\n&= (e + 2) - 2 \\\\\n&= e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 false] [17592186133617 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133618 1100 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, 1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1$.\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" 13194139535206 false] [17592186133618 1100 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133618 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\nabla f\n$$\n\nSince we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= ye^{xy}, \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= xe^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨0, 1⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$.\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= f(1, 0) - f(0, 1) \\\\\n&= e^{1 \\cdot 0} - e^{0 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= 1 - 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 false] [17592186133618 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133629 1100 "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$." 13194139535206 false] [17592186133629 1100 "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133629 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C$. A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1]\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨ - 1, 2⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1)$.\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(-1)) \\\\\n&= f(1, 1) - f(-1, 2) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot e^{1} - (-1) \\cdot e^{2} \\\\\n&= e + e^{2} \\\\\n&= e(1 + e)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 false] [17592186133629 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot {e}^{1}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =e+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =e(1+e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186133801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337479 13194139535206 true] [17592186133801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337521 13194139535206 true] [17592186133801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337528 13194139535206 true] [17592186133801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337535 13194139535206 true] [17592186133801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337542 13194139535206 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337486 13194139535206 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337493 13194139535206 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337500 13194139535206 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337507 13194139535206 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337514 13194139535206 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337472 13194139535206 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337549 13194139535206 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337556 13194139535206 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337563 13194139535206 true] [17592186134002 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535206 true] [17592186134002 1100 "Let $f(x, y) = y^{2} - x^{2}$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y^{2} = 4x$ for $y ∈ [0, 2\\sqrt{2}]$, evaluate $∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$." 13194139535206 false] [17592186134002 1100 "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=4x$ for $y\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186134002 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\sqrt{2}) & =⟨2,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\sqrt{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,2\\sqrt{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =((2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-{2}^{2})-({0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(8-4)-(0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337472 1017 #edb/ref 17592186337473 13194139535206 true] [17592186337472 1089 "q-104617" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337472 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535206 true] [17592186337472 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337472 1113 #uuid "d9b52684-6943-5924-8a3c-2cbafe021e4b" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337472 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337474 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337475 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337476 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337477 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337478 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1041 "selection" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1104 #edb/ref 17592186337477 13194139535206 true] [17592186337473 1110 #uuid "9d28a1bb-c094-553f-b16c-0efb855f6537" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337474 1034 "\\pi -1" 13194139535206 true] [17592186337474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1319413953